 Question #2:

R has actually lots of built-in datasets.
For example, the rivers data “gives the lengths (in miles) of 141 “major” rivers in North America, as compiled by the US Geological Survey”.
Use data(rivers)
```{r}
# TODO: Load the built-in rivers dataset
data(rivers)
```

Get the number of elements (length) in rivers, their sum (sum), mean (mean), median(median), variance(var), standard deviation(sd), minimum (min) and maximum (max).
(Your answer here)
```{r}
# TODO: Get the length, sum, mean, median, variance, standard deviation,
# minimum and maximum of rivers
length(rivers)
sum(rivers)
mean(rivers)
median(rivers)
var(rivers)
sd(rivers)
min(rivers)
max(rivers)
```



What is the result? The derived result must be in just one(1) vector name.

```{r}
# TODO: Put all the results above in just one (1) vector name
rivers_stats <- c(length = length(rivers),
                  sum    = sum(rivers),
                  mean   = mean(rivers),
                  median = median(rivers),
                  var    = var(rivers),
                  sd     = sd(rivers),
                  min    = min(rivers),
                  max    = max(rivers))
print(rivers_stats)
```

